X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Meetup: A Book Club Downtown": "https://www.meetup.com/a-book-club-downtown-abcd/events/313229064/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:meetup_book_club_abcd
X-SOURCE:Meetup: A Book Club Downtown
UID:event_313229064@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260303T215736Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260414T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260414T210000
SUMMARY:ABCD Reads "Interpreter of Maladies" by Jhumpa Lahiri
DESCRIPTION:A Book Club\, Downtown (ABCD)\nABCD Reads "**Interpreter of Ma
 ladies**" by Jhumpa Lahiri\, a short story collection that won the Pulitze
 r Prize for Fiction in 2000.\n\n[[RESULTS](https://opavote.com/results/516
 5914897252352) of the poll for this event]\n\n**:: ABOUT THE BOOK ::**\n"N
 avigating between the Indian traditions they've inherited and the baffling
  new world\, the characters in Jhumpa Lahiri's elegant\, touching stories 
 seek love beyond the barriers of culture and generations. In "A Temporary 
 Matter\," published in The New Yorker\, a young Indian-American couple fac
 es the heartbreak of a stillborn birth while their Boston neighborhood cop
 es with a nightly blackout. In the title story\, an interpreter guides an 
 American family through the India of their ancestors and hears an astonish
 ing confession. Lahiri writes with deft cultural insight reminiscent of An
 ita Desai and a nuanced depth that recalls Mavis Gallant." (from [Goodread
 s](https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/5439.Interpreter_of_Maladies))\n\n"
 With accomplished precision and gentle eloquence\, Jhumpa Lahiri traces th
 e crosscurrents set in motion when immigrants\, expatriates\, and their ch
 ildren arrive\, quite literally\, at a cultural divide.\nA blackout forces
  a young Indian American couple to make confessions that unravel their tat
 tered domestic peace. An Indian American girl recognizes her cultural iden
 tity during a Halloween celebration while the Pakastani civil war rages on
  television in the background. A latchkey kid with a single working mother
  finds affinity with a woman from Calcutta. In the title story\, an interp
 reter guides an American family through the India of their ancestors and h
 ears an astonishing confession.\nImbued with the sensual details of Indian
  culture\, these stories speak with passion and wisdom to everyone who has
  ever felt like a foreigner. Like the interpreter of the title story\, Lah
 iri translates between the strict traditions of her ancestors and a baffli
 ng new world." (from the [Toronto Public Library](https://tpl.bibliocommon
 s.com/v2/record/S234C194772))\n\n**:: ABOUT THE EVENT ::**\nYou are welcom
 e to arrive 30 minutes before the event and stay 30 minutes or longer afte
 rward to connect with other attendees outside the book talk.\n\nPlease ens
 ure you have read the book before attending (though finishing any book is 
 not a barrier to participating\, as long as you don't mind spoilers).\n\nF
 irst-time attendees can join for free. If the event is full\, we maintain 
 an unofficial waitlist. Please send [Michael](https://www.meetup.com/membe
 rs/55295192/) a direct message for more information\, in either case.\n\nW
 e request a $3 contribution from regular attendees to help cover our month
 ly meetup costs.\n\n**:: ABOUT OUR NEXT BOOK ::**\nAt the end of each even
 t\, we will ask for nominees for our next book. If you have a good one in 
 mind\, please ensure it is between 200-400 pages long\, available from the
  Toronto Public Library\, and counts as contemporary literary fiction (tho
 ugh these are not hard and fast rules). When enough nominees are collected
 \, Michael will send the members a direct message with a link where you ca
 n vote.
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/a-book-club-downtown-abcd/events/3132
 29064/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260206T000238Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260206T000238Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"City of Toronto Festivals & Events": "https://www.toronto.ca/explore-enjoy/history-art-culture/cultural-centres-galleries/assembly-hall/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:toronto_festivals
SUMMARY:A Daughter’s Place by Martha Batiz - By the Lake Book Club 
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-04:00";VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260414T190000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-04:00";VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260414T190000
UID:6d82e23fb3166d9e7faeb9568c79741d@toronto.ca
DESCRIPTION:A Daughter’s Place\, the debut novel by award-winning author
  Martha Bátiz\, is a sweeping historical narrative set in the Spanish Gol
 den Age. Inspired by the real-life daughter of Miguel de Cervantes\, the b
 ook follows fifteen-year-old Isabel as she navigates secrecy\, love\, and 
 survival in a patriarchal world while her father works on Don Quixote. Thr
 ough richly detailed prose\, Bátiz gives voice to the women who lived in 
 the shadows of history\, blending drama\, resilience and untold stories.\n
 \nBy the Lake Book Club hosts six Tuesday-night events from February to De
 cember with top Canadian authors and Humber Polytechnics alumni. Each in-p
 erson event features readings\, discussions\, Q&A\, book signings and ASL 
 interpretation\, while virtual sessions include live subtitles and audio a
 ccess.\nCategories: Literary
LOCATION:Assembly Hall\, 1 Colonel Samuel Smith Park Dr\, Etobicoke\, ON\,
  M8V 4B6
URL:https://www.toronto.ca/explore-enjoy/history-art-culture/cultural-cent
 res-galleries/assembly-hall/
X-SOURCE:City of Toronto Festivals & Events
END:VEVENT
