BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a201ea8089f23c675eb009"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_events
SUMMARY:Music Copyright and Ethics in the AI Era
DTSTART;TZID=UTC;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260522T180000Z
DTEND;TZID=UTC;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260522T200000Z
UID:69a201ea8089f23c675eb009@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:When you use AI to create music\, who actually owns it? Can a 
 melody generated by a machine infringe on someone else's copyright? As AI 
 tools become more accessible\, these questions are more relevant than ever
  - and the answers aren't always straightforward.\nIn this session\, you'l
 l get a plain-language breakdown of how music copyright works\, what the r
 ules are around sampling\, and how these long-standing principles apply to
  AI-generated music. You'll also hear about landmark legal cases - both su
 rprising victories and cautionary tales - including real-world examples fr
 om a working composer's own career.\nDrop-in. No registration required.\nT
 his program is part of the Innovator in Residence (AI and Music Production
 ) program\, which is designed to help beginner and intermediate learners b
 uild knowledge and creative skills and get hands-on with current AI tools 
 in music production and learn about the history\, copyright and ethics of 
 generative music. For more information and a full list of programs\, visit
 \ntpl.ca/airesident\n.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Room 201\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69a201ea8089f23c675eb009
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6961315491e4576f847ba0a1"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_events
SUMMARY:LEAP After School Program
DTSTART;TZID=UTC;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260522T190000Z
DTEND;TZID=UTC;VALUE=DATE-TIME:20260522T210000Z
UID:6961315491e4576f847ba0a1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This  program offers a fun and engaging environment with two 
 hours of physical activities\, trivia\, games\, crafts\, and sports-based 
 activities. Participants will build independence and social skills while e
 njoying nutritious snacks and educational workshops. The program is safe\,
  supervised\, and facilitated in partnership with North York Community Hou
 se LEAP staff.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information o
 n this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Li
 braries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will onl
 y be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Qu
 estions about how the library handles your information can be directed to 
 Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street
 \, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Woodview Park\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6961315491e4576f847ba0a1
